  "prediction": "The Department of Defense (DOD) has policies and procedures in place to prevent and respond to unwanted sexual behaviors, including sexual assault, sexual harassment, and domestic violence. However, the report highlights several areas for improvement.\n\nThe report notes that DOD's policies on sexual harassment do not fully include principles from the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention's (CDC) framework for preventing sexual violence. Specifically, the policies lack risk and protective factors, risk domains, and tertiary strategies for prevention. The report also finds that DOD's policies on sexual harassment do not consistently include mechanisms for anonymous reporting, as required by the National Defense Authorization Act for FY 2013.\n\nThe report also examines DOD's data collection efforts on unwanted sexual behaviors. While DOD has reliable data systems for collecting and reporting data on sexual assault and domestic violence involving sexual assault, inconsistencies in data elements and definitions among the military services create difficulties in developing department-wide reports on unwanted sexual behaviors.\n\nThe report makes four recommendations to DOD:\n\n1.  The Under Secretary of Defense for Personnel and Readiness should fully include in the new policy for sexual harassment the principles in the CDC's framework for sexual violence prevention, including risk and protective factors, risk domains, and tertiary strategies.\n2.  The Under Secretary of Defense for Personnel and Readiness should include in the new policy for sexual harassment mechanisms for anonymous reporting of incidents consistent with section 579 of the National Defense Authorization Act for FY 2013.\n3.  The Under Secretary of Defense for Personnel and Readiness should direct the Office of Diversity Management and Equal Opportunity to develop standard data elements and definitions for maintaining and reporting information on sexual harassment incidents at the military service level, and direct the military services to incorporate these data elements and definitions into their military service-specific databases.\n4.  The Under Secretary of Defense for Personnel and Readiness should direct the Assistant Secretary of Defense for Readiness to incorporate in its continuum of harm prevention strategy all the elements that are key for establishing a long-term, results-oriented strategic planning framework.\n\nThe report concludes that DOD has made progress in addressing unwanted sexual behaviors, but more work is needed to ensure that the department's policies and procedures are effective in preventing and responding to these behaviors.",
